          "id": 0,
          "code": "def valid_date(date):\n    if not date:\n        return False\n    \n    parts = date.split('-')\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return False\n    \n    mm_str, dd_str, yyyy_str = parts\n    \n    # Check format: mm-dd-yyyy implies exactly 3 parts separated by '-'\n    # Also need to ensure no extra characters or empty parts\n    if not mm_str or not dd_str or not yyyy_str:\n        return False\n    \n    try:\n        mm = int(mm_str)\n        dd = int(dd_str)\n        yyyy = int(yyyy_str)\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n    \n    # Rule 3: Months 1-12\n    if mm < 1 or mm > 12:\n        return False\n    \n    # Rule 2: Days limits\n    # Months 1,3,5,7,8,10,12 -> 31 days\n    # Months 4,6,9,11 -> 30 days\n    # Month 2 -> 29 days (simplified per spec, ignoring leap year logic not mentioned)\n    \n    if mm in [1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12]:\n        max_days = 31\n    elif mm in [4, 6, 9, 11]:\n        max_days = 30\n    elif mm == 2:\n        max_days = 29\n    else:\n        return False\n    \n    if dd < 1 or dd > max_days:\n        return False\n    \n    # Additional check for leading zeros/format strictness if implied by examples\n    # The spec says format mm-dd-yyyy. Usually implies 2 digits for mm/dd.\n    # Example '15-01-2012' is False (15 is not a month), but '06-04-2020' is True.\n    # Example '04-0-2040' is False (day 0 is invalid).\n    # However, '3-11-2000' vs '03-11-2000': The example uses '03'.\n    # Strict format usually requires 2 digits for mm and dd.\n    if len(mm_str) != 2 or len(dd_str) != 2:\n        # Check if it's just a number representation issue or strict format.\n        # The example '15-01-2012' failed because 15 is not a month.\n        # The example '04-0-2040' failed because day is 0.\n        # Let's check if the string representation matches the integer without leading zeros.\n        # If input is '3-11-2000', int('3') is 3. But format mm usually implies 2 chars.\n        # Given '03-11-2000' is True, strict 2-digit is likely required.\n        return False\n    \n    return True\n",
